Subject: [PATCH] winemetal, d3d11: shared textures and events leave launchd with their object (cranvr)

DXMT registered the Mach port of each shared texture of its stock path (MTLSharedTextureHandle: formats other than
RGBA8 and BGRA8, mipmaps, arrays, MSAA, legacy handles) and of each shared event (a shared ID3D11Fence, the event of
the keyed mutex of patch 0014) with launchd under the object's shared name, for OpenSharedResource and
OpenSharedFence in another process. These ports belong to the kernel and never die. launchd kept them, with the
memory of the textures, until the user logged out. launchd also refuses to remove a name (a register with no port
answers 1101, BOOTSTRAP_NAME_IN_USE). On 2026-11-27 one run of SteamVR left 30 names with 240 MB in launchd.

winemetal now shares such a port through a broker. WMTBootstrapRegister makes a service port whose receive right the
process holds, one for each object. It registers that port under the name. A thread of the broker answers each
request on a service port with a copy of the object's send right. WMTBootstrapUnregister, a new call, destroys the
service port when the object goes. launchd then drops the name at once (0.2 ms in a check). The end of the process
destroys the service port too. WMTBootstrapLookUp asks the service port for the object's port, with a time-out of a
second. WMTMachPortDeallocate, a new call, frees a right that the look-up gave.

A texture of the stock path cannot go by the global ID of its IOSurface, as the IOSurface textures of patch 0019 do.
Metal makes it on an IOSurface that is not global: IOSurfaceLookup in another process finds nothing (checked for
RGBA8, RGBA16Float, mipmaps, two slices, 4x MSAA, Depth32Float, BC1 and RGB10A2). The surface also reports no size
and no pixel format. Its port is the only way into another process. An event has no ID at all. The private calls
stay those that DXMT used before (initWithMachPort: of MTLSharedTextureHandle, newSharedEventWithMachPort: of the
device, eventPort of MTLSharedEventHandle). A check in two native processes opened all eight textures above and an
event through the broker: each texture had its description, the RGBA8 one had its pixels, the event had its value.
Neither call takes the caller's right, so the caller frees it.

- A shared texture of the stock path is served while its DeviceTexture lives. The allocation frees its own right with
  the texture, in the process that made it and in each process that opened it. DXMT kept those rights until the
  process ended. A process that shares an opened texture again (patch 0018) serves its own port under a new name.
  The new private data carries that name in server_name, so the next process does not depend on the creator. The
  unique name stays the same in every process, because the keyed mutex names its section by it.
- A shared fence is served while the fence lives. The event of a keyed mutex is served while the process that made
  its section holds the section. A process that comes later gets no event. Its keyed mutex then takes the first way
  (the writer's ReleaseSync waits for its GPU).
- An opened texture and an opened shared buffer (patch 0013) kept one reference too many. Com takes the first
  reference itself. ref() took a second one. They stayed with their memory until the process ended. The same held
  for the IOSurfaces of patch 0019 that another DXMT process opened.
- The resource deleted its keyed mutex through IDXGIKeyedMutex, which has no virtual destructor. The keyed mutex's
  section and the name of its event therefore stayed until the process ended. The resource now holds the concrete
  type.
- The import of an IOSurface texture compares the width, the height and the pixel format of the surface that the ID
  names with the runtime data. It refuses another surface with a log line. macOS gives the lowest free ID to a new
  surface, so the ID of a released texture names the next new surface at once.

In Wine 11.10 a shared fence still does not open in another process, as before. DXMT's CreateFence stores its name
with D3DKMT_ESCAPE_UPDATE_RESOURCE_WINE, which Wine runs for resources only. DXMT's check of the result is the wrong
way round, so the fence goes on without the name. OpenSharedFence reads the name with
D3DKMTQueryResourceInfoFromNtHandle, which Wine answers for resources only. The fence's name in launchd now goes with
the fence all the same.

scripts/shared-churn.sh in cranvr runs runtime/tests/shared_churn.exe (runtime patch 0017). In each cycle it makes six
textures of the stock path, one IOSurface texture and a shared fence, which a second process opens and checks. Before
the patch, 2 cycles of 64 x 64 left 16 names in launchd with 375 KB of surfaces after both processes had ended. The
handle of a released IOSurface texture opened on a new surface of another size. With the patch, 200 cycles of
640 x 333 show 8 names while both processes hold the objects. None stays after each cycle or after the end. The
IOSurfaces of both processes grow by 11 MB once while the cycles make 1981 MB.
